= Introduction <sec:intro>

== Motivation <sec:intro-motivation>

*Warehouse fleets jam.* Automated warehouses move goods with fleets of mobile robots that share narrow aisles, intersections and pick stations. The planning literature treats the warehouse as a stream of path finding problems, in which robots receive new goals as soon as they finish old ones, and even there congestion is a first-order concern: planners that route each robot along its free-flow shortest path create congestion themselves @chen2023traffic, and a solver that plans over a bounded horizon can leave two agents in a corridor waiting for each other @li2020lifelong. When robots meet head-on in an aisle, or several arrive at a junction together, the fleet can stop moving altogether. A deadlock of this kind does not dissolve on its own: each robot holds the floor it stands on while it waits for floor held by another, which is the structure of the classical "deadly embrace" @coffman1971system.

*Local reactive control cannot break the symmetry.* In practice, many robots navigate with a local stack such as Nav2 @macenski2020marathon, which plans for one robot at a time and treats other robots as obstacles. Reactive methods of this kind keep each robot safe, but they decide how to avoid a collision, not who should give way. A planner can conclude that every path forward is unsafe and stop, although a feasible path exists @trautman2010unfreezing. For controllers built on control barrier functions, deadlock has been characterised as an equilibrium of the closed-loop dynamics on the boundary of the safe set @grover2019deadlock: the robots stop precisely because continuing would violate safety. Two robots that face each other and run the same recovery policy can recreate the stall they are trying to escape (@sec:bg-reactive).

*A slow semantic layer could supply what is missing.* The remedies proposed for reactive deadlock share one structure. A slower decision layer sets an order or a timing, and a faster layer keeps the motion safe (@sec:bg-reactive). What such a layer needs is a view of the whole situation: where the robots stand, which of them blocks which, and what else occupies the aisle. A fixed overhead camera sees exactly that. Vision-language models (VLMs), which read an image together with a text description and answer in a structured form, are candidates for turning that view into a decision. They can name the kind of jam and choose a way out from a closed vocabulary of strategies, such as letting one robot yield or clearing a junction in phases. A closely related line of work already calls a VLM on a stall, shows it a top-view image, and uses it as a high-level planner above a safe low-level controller @garg2024foundation @garg2026online.

*A model's plan cannot be trusted as it stands.* Language models do not plan reliably on their own @valmeekam2023planning. The position that has emerged is to treat their output as a candidate that external, model-based checks must verify @kambhampati2024llms. A VLM is also slow, costly per call and fallible: it may time out, refuse, return malformed output, or pick a robot to yield that has no room to back up. The design question is therefore not only whether a VLM can recognise a jam, but how its decision can be turned into commands that are safe to execute, and how much of the executed plan can still be credited to the model. These two questions run through the whole report.

== Problem Statement <sec:intro-problem>

This report studies a hybrid, two-layer coordinator for a warehouse robot fleet. Layer 1 is fast and local: each robot navigates with its own Nav2 stack, with a design target of 20–50 Hz #ev("1.01"). Layer 2 is slow and central, with a design target of 0.5–2 Hz #ev("1.01"). When a stall detector reports that robots have stopped making progress, an orchestrator sends the annotated overhead frame and the tracked robot states to a VLM. The VLM classifies the congestion and chooses a strategy from a closed set of eight #ev("1.02"). A deterministic grounding layer turns that decision into robot commands, and an independent safety monitor enforces robot-to-robot distance and speed limits on every command, with one exemption for clearing manoeuvres that @sec:design-safety describes; it does not check clearance to static obstacles (@sec:design).

The grounding layer is a deterministic, geometry-driven grounder that checks the model's decision against the scene (with one known gap: only the yielder's retreat room is checked against static obstacles, not escort segments or reverse legs; @sec:design-grounding), repairs or replaces an infeasible plan, and refuses to move robots when no safe manoeuvre exists (@sec:design-grounding). One property of this design shapes every claim in the report: the command the robots execute is always the grounder's output. The one exception is an opt-in switch that lets a verified model plan set the order of the back-out phases; it was used only in the demonstration campaign (Chapter 6). The grounder also audits any scene-wide plan the model proposes and labels it as matched verbatim, repaired, or replaced by the grounder's own default plan (its provenance label). These labels record how the model's proposal fared in the grounder's audit. They do not show which commands came from the model: in the evaluated runtime the grounder never reads the proposed plan, none of the 20 ER-2 `junction_clear` plans at the intersection was accepted unchanged, and 6 of the 14 accepted four-robot plans, most of them from an earlier gpt-4o pilot, were not dispatched as written #ev("6.02").

The problem addressed is therefore the following. _Can a pipeline in which a stall trigger detects congestion and a VLM, given the annotated overhead frame and a text list of robot states, confirms and classifies it, and a deterministic layer grounds its decision into safe commands, clear congestion that local reactive control leaves in place, and what does each part of the pipeline contribute?_ The question is posed for a small simulated warehouse fleet and answered with one registered pilot, exploratory runs and post hoc decision-level analyses. Any success is credited to the pipeline (VLM and grounding together), not to the model alone.

The project proposal framed a broader comparison against classical geometric coordinators. The study narrowed as the work progressed: the final experiments compare the pipeline with Layer 2 switched off on the same code, and with a scripted arm that skips the VLM call, is handed the strategy and robot set, and runs the grounder's default plan. @sec:intro-scope and Chapter 5 give the reasons.

== Research Questions <sec:intro-rq>

The research design frozen with the first preregistration states five research questions #ev("1.03"). They are defined in @sec:ch5-rq and repeated here in short form.

- *RQ1, perception and classification.* Does the VLM classify the congestion type and the kind of blocker correctly from the annotated scene, and does it tell congestion apart from normal flow? Because the VLM is called only after the stall trigger fires, RQ1 concerns post-trigger classification and false positives, not open-world detection.
- *RQ2, strategy competence.* Do the strategies the VLM chooses clear congestion?
- *RQ3, social reasoning.* Are the robot the VLM names to yield, and the crossing order it proposes, effective?
- *RQ4, semantic advantage.* Does the VLM use information that no classical baseline has, such as what a blocker is?
- *RQ5, closed-loop adaptation.* Does feedback about a failed command change the VLM's next decision? (Here "closed loop" means re-analysis that receives feedback about the failed command, not the pipeline cycle defined in @sec:intro-contributions.)

These questions frame the report. The experiments that were finally run answer narrower questions, which Chapter 5 states with each experiment (@sec:ch5-arms). The three live comparisons, the offline bench, the demonstration campaign and the offline replays and traces are kept strictly apart and never pooled.

- *prereg-v3, tranche 1 (pilot).* In a five-robot task-stream scene #ev("1.14"), how large is the effect of switching Layer 2 on, against switching it off, on task completion, throughput, delay and per-conflict decision quality, and how uncertain is that estimate? No directional hypothesis was registered. Tranche 1 ran 5 seeds per arm, 10 episodes in all, against a registered 20 per arm #ev("1.08"); because tranche 2 was not run, it is reported as a *pilot*. A descriptive classification of why the episodes that did not finish stalled, by stage of the pipeline, is added as an exploratory analysis (@sec:res-failure-causes); it is not part of the registered question.
- *IB10, Layer 2 on against off (exploratory).* In a four-robot intersection, does the full Layer 2 pipeline physically clear a jam that stays frozen when Layer 2 is off? It ran 5 episodes per arm #ev("1.09").
- *IB10, grounder default (exploratory).* In a second, separate run on the same intersection, does the jam clear in the same way when no VLM is called and the plan is the grounder's default? That arm had 5 slots #ev("1.10").
- *Offline bench and live shadow (exploratory).* How do prompt and input variants for the VLM rank on recorded scenes, judged by what the real grounder would do with their answers? A later per-cluster variant also ran as a decision-only live shadow in 7 episodes #ev("1.11").
- *Head-on decisions (decision level).* A re-score of every recorded head-on decision and three pre-registered offline replays ask whether the model's choice of yielder follows the scene #ev("6.08") #ev("6.09") #ev("6.10") #ev("6.11").
- *Plan influence (decision level).* A trace of every live four-robot analysis asks whether the model's plan changed what was dispatched #ev("6.02").
- *Demonstration campaign (illustrative).* Twelve valid live takes in four scenes, with each scene's earlier grounding settings and the safety settings of the second IB10 run, show what the pipeline does; no rate is computed #ev("6.01").

