The experiments therefore do not test every registered question. RQ4 and RQ5 are not tested by any experiment reported here. RQ2 and RQ3 are not tested against the comparators registered for them at prereg-v1 (a per-type policy table, and a first-come-first-served rule with a commitment tiebreak), because from prereg-v3 the control is Layer 2 switched off. RQ3 is informed only at the decision level, by the bench, the presentation-order finding from the pilots (@sec:intro-contributions), a re-score of all recorded head-on decisions, and pre-registered offline replays that asked whether the model's choice of yielder follows the scene; no tested model met the pre-registered rule #ev("6.08") #ev("6.09"). Because the grounder may repair or replace the model's plan, every live outcome is reported together with the plan-provenance label of its dispatches and, for the four-robot scenes, a re-grounding of each decision without its plan, because the label alone does not show which executed commands came from the model.

== Contributions <sec:intro-contributions>

The contributions are of two kinds. The system and method contributions are artefacts that exist and have been run, and they are stated plainly. The empirical findings come from pilots and offline analysis, and each is stated with the kind of evidence behind it. Outcomes of the main experiments are reported in Chapter 6.

*System and method contributions.*

+ *A working closed loop in simulation.* Here and below, "closed loop" means the full cycle of detect, decide, ground, act and resolve, run in simulation. The coordinator runs end to end in Isaac Sim with ROS 2, Nav2 and a hosted VLM. An overhead camera with ArUco tracking produces robot states, a stall detector decides when to ask, the VLM decides, the grounder produces commands, and an execution layer carries them out and reports back (Chapters 3 and 4). Layer 2 can be switched off by one configuration key, which gives every experiment a Layer-1-only control on identical code (@sec:design-modules).
+ *A deterministic grounding layer with plan provenance.* The grounder checks each decision against the scene geometry and the robot positions. It hands a yield to another robot when the named yielder has no room to back up, refuses when no robot has, and audits the model's proposed plan with a bounded repair search. Every dispatch carries a provenance label and degradation tags (@sec:design-grounding). The label records the audit of the model's plan; which executed commands came from the model is established separately, by re-grounding each decision without its plan and comparing (Chapter 6). Deterministic verify-and-repair of model plans is not new on its own (@sec:bg-grounding). What is, as far as we found, not reported by the closest published systems is this per-dispatch provenance as an experimental quantity (@sec:bg-comparison).
+ *A safety layer that does not depend on the model.* In the evaluated system, the model's choice passes through the same safety checks as any other command, except for the clearing-manoeuvre exemption, whose scope proved wider than intended (@sec:design-safety). An independent monitor holds any pair of robots closer than 0.45 m and any robot faster than its speed limit, and treats a robot whose marker is lost as unsafe. In the live experiments it adds a hard-contact floor at 0.20 m that applies even inside the exemption that clearing manoeuvres need #ev("1.04"). The floor is armed per run and checked by the run procedure around every run (@sec:design-safety). That wider scope was found by a review after the experiments, and the evaluated runtime does not contain the fix. The floor acts on the centre-to-centre distance of tracked robot pairs. Neither the monitor nor the grounder checked escort segments or reverse legs against static obstacles, and in one demonstration take an escort drove a robot into a rack island (Chapter 6) #ev("6.01").
+ *Preregistration discipline.* The experimental design was registered in three versions, the first frozen with a repository tag on 2026-09-30 and the third dated 2026-10-06 #ev("1.05"). Later changes were made as numbered, append-only addenda. In the registered experiments, every scored criterion was fixed before the data it scores were collected, the post hoc analyses of Chapter 6 are labelled as such, and pilot episodes were excluded from confirmatory counts (@sec:ch5-prereg).
+ *An offline replay bench.* The bench replays 187 recorded analyses from 31 live episodes #ev("1.06"), re-grounds each answer with the production grounder, and scores it against ground-truth conflict definitions. It ranks prompt and input variants for the VLM by what would happen downstream, without simulating any episode (@sec:ch5-bench).
+ *A physical demonstration.* The detect-to-dispatch chain also runs on real robots. On 12 November 2026, the perception, reasoning, grounding and dispatch chain ran on physical TurtleBot3 robots under an overhead camera, in four staged arrangements of up to four robots #ev("1.07"). The demonstration used a different reasoning model from the simulation experiments, the robots had no navigation goals, one run per arrangement was recorded, and its code is not part of this repository's history (@sec:impl-physical). It shows that the chain runs on real hardware; it is not an evaluation.

*Evaluations performed.* The report carries out the three live comparisons and the offline bench listed in @sec:intro-rq, each with its registered measures, safety gates and plan-provenance labels, and adds the decision-level analyses and the demonstration campaign listed there. Their outcomes are reported in Chapter 6; in short, and bounded as the evidence register's claims ladder bounds them #ev("6.16"):

- *prereg-v3 tranche 1 (pilot).* No episode in either arm completed all 25 tasks (0 of 10). The throughput difference, Layer 2 on minus off, was +0.032 with a bootstrap interval of [−0.008, +0.080] that includes zero; with five seed pairs this is a pilot estimate, not a confirmatory result #ev("6.14"). An exploratory classification assigns each failure unit (8 in Arm A, 10 in Arm B, where no command is dispatched) a primary cause class, including undetermined, as @tab:ch6-fc-primary shows; all 10 Arm B units are undetermined, because their execution logs cannot be placed on the simulation clock. It is post hoc and changes no outcome #ev("6.63").
- *IB10, Layer 2 on against off.* In the simulated four-robot intersection jam, the full Layer 2 pipeline (congestion trigger, VLM call, grounding and phased dispatch) cleared the jam in 4 of 5 episodes, against 0 of 5 with Layer 2 off #ev("6.05").
- *IB10, grounder default.* In a separate run, the live-VLM arm cleared it in 5 of 5 (4 of 4 without the smoke episode, whose acceptance deviated from the registered procedure) and a scripted arm that skipped the VLM call and ran the grounder's default plan in 4 of 4 valid episodes (5 of 5 after a post hoc re-run), in similar time; every first dispatch of the live-VLM arm re-grounds to exactly the scripted arm's plan. Both runs are exploratory pilots, n = 5 per arm, not independent samples, and never pooled #ev("6.06").
- *Head-on decisions.* In the two-robot head-on scenes, the grounded yielder was made correct by the grounder's feasibility gate, which overturned 37 of 79 model choices, not by the model, whose choice never beat the better of two trivial baselines #ev("6.08"). In pre-registered offline replays, no tested model met the pre-registered rule for choosing the yielder from the scene rather than from robot labels or listing order, and several cells could not be assessed #ev("6.09").
- *Plan influence.* A trace of the live four-robot analyses found that the model's plan changed the dispatched commands in 0 of 39 analyses that pass its fidelity gate #ev("6.02").
- *Demonstration campaign (illustrative).* Gemini Robotics-ER 2 returned a valid, parseable decision in all 67 analyses of the campaign, but its plan was recorded as passing the audit unchanged in only 6 of 65 plan-channel analyses; after an offline correction that checks the command actually sent against the plan, 5 of those 6 were accepted and dispatched as written #ev("6.44") #ev("6.51"). In the intersection scene, the only one where this was computed, it changed the executed commands relative to the grounder's default once, by swapping the order of two near-equivalent back-out manoeuvres. Safety evidence is limited to robot-pair centre distance; one sustained robot-obstacle contact, caused by an escort path that had no obstacle check, was confirmed offline from ground-truth poses and the recording #ev("6.01").
- *Offline bench and live shadow.* On 137 recorded positives, text only, a per-cluster answer format raised cluster coverage from 0.496 to 0.832 for ER-2 and from 0.620 to 0.934 for Sonnet 5.5, but a chooser that takes the first listed option reached 0.949, and on involvement the models match and do not beat this default ordering #ev("6.46"). The per-cluster call ran live as a shadow beside the ordinary loop in 7 episodes; all 50 shadow blocks were valid, and the shadow answers were never dispatched #ev("6.47").

*Empirical findings from pilots and offline analysis.*

- *Presentation order.* In pilot probes, the robot that one model named to yield followed the robot listed first in the prompt, which under the default ascending order was also the robot with the lowest identifier (the two priors are confounded), rather than the geometry of the scene. The finding changed the first preregistration: from its amendments on, the order in which robots are listed is drawn at random, seeded and recorded, and analyses condition on it #ev("1.12"). The later live cells reported in this report (tranche 1 and both IB10 runs) nevertheless listed the robots in ascending identifier order, as every decision artifact records #ev("1.15"), so their yielder choices cannot separate the two priors. A later pre-registered replay that swapped the robots' labels confirmed the finding for gpt-4o: its choice moved with the listing, not with the scene #ev("6.09").
- *The grounder carries the plan.* In the earlier live pilot on the intersection scene, every plan the model proposed was repaired or replaced by the grounder #ev("1.13"). This observation is why the claims of this report are worded about the pipeline, and why every outcome is reported with its plan-provenance label. A later trace of all live four-robot analyses found that the model's plan changed the dispatched commands in 0 of 39 #ev("6.02"). In the demonstration campaign, with an opt-in switch on, it changed them once, by swapping the order of two near-equivalent back-out manoeuvres #ev("6.01").
- *Which stage limits the pipeline.* The report asks which stage limits the pipeline, using decision-only re-grounding of recorded live analyses, the offline bench, and a classification of the primary cause of each failure unit of the five-robot scene by stage (decision, grounding and verification, execution, environment geometry). The classification is exploratory, and its counts are reported per stratum without pooling. In the five-robot scene, re-grounding the recorded live analyses showed that 78 analyses gave 13 motion commands: 47 were withheld by deterministic grounding refusals, and one refusal class, the static gate of `junction_clear`, accounted for 27 of the 47 #ev("6.15"). The demonstration campaign shows the same pattern: in the five-robot take, 6 of 8 decisions were withheld by grounding refusals, and in the first three convoy takes the 33 later proposals were all grounded as 8 s waits #ev("6.01"). The evidence is decision-only re-grounding and illustrative takes, not a controlled comparison; it shows where proposals were withheld, not which stage caused the episodes that did not finish. The classification names no dominant cause: the two raters' own codes put different classes first in tranche 1 Arm A and in the pilot stratum as a whole, and 27 of the 55 failure units, about half, stay undetermined (@sec:res-failure-causes) #ev("6.63"). Which stage limits the pipeline is therefore not settled by this report.

== Scope and Non-Goals <sec:intro-scope>

The report is bounded in the following ways.

- *One overhead camera, planar poses.* The system uses a single fixed overhead camera and estimates positions and headings on the ground plane only. Multi-camera fusion and full six-degree-of-freedom pose estimation are out of scope.
- *Conflict clearing, not global planning.* The coordinator does not replan the fleet as a multi-agent path finding solver would @stern2019multi. It handles one detected conflict at a time with a small set of bounded manoeuvres and then hands control back to the robots' own planners. Layer 2 is not in the fast (20–50 Hz) control loop #ev("1.01"); it acts only on detected stalls.
- *No classical coordinator as control.* The proposal planned comparisons with potential fields, reciprocal collision avoidance and conflict-based search. The final experiments do not include them. Their control is Layer 2 switched off on the same code, so they measure pipeline on against pipeline off, not a VLM against a classical coordinator (@sec:ch5-threats). The measures registered for the final design (@sec:ch5-measures) do not include energy use, which the proposal had listed.
- *Simulation for every scored episode.* All scored episodes ran in Isaac Sim, each experiment in one scene, with one model in the live comparisons and one pinned runtime. The physical demonstration is not part of the experiments.
- *Small samples and exploratory status.* The live comparisons have five episodes or seed pairs per arm #ev("1.08") #ev("1.09"). Only prereg-v3 was registered as confirmatory, and its single tranche is reported as a pilot. The IB10 runs, the bench and the shadow run are exploratory. The failure-cause classification covers 33 episodes in three strata of 10, 21 and 2 episodes that are never pooled. The demonstration campaign has one to five takes per scene and is illustrative only; no rate is computed from it #ev("6.01"). No result is generalised beyond its scene, model and runtime.
- *Later extensions are not evaluated.* Variants built after the pinned runtime (grounding-first prompts, per-cluster choice, refusal feedback, the fix to the safety exemption and the later safeguards) are off by default and appear only as exploratory work. The safety-exemption fix was switched on in IB10 run 2, where it never fired, and in the demonstration takes, where the registration required it on M4's start-up line and its firings were not counted; neither is a live validation. A later prompt that lists the actions the verifier accepts, with the plan-order switch, ran only in the demonstration campaign. An escort obstacle guard, geometric contact logging, an execution-side stall abort and a hold for queued robots in the convoy scene were built afterwards. The guard ran live once, in a validation take, armed but never triggered; the contact logging is only partially validated; the stall abort has not run live; and the queued-robot hold was used in two demonstration convoy takes, which stayed unresolved (@sec:impl-later).
- *Safety is bounded.* The safety gates bound the centre distance between robot pairs in the episodes reported, and no more. The simulator's contact fields were a stub that always logged an empty list, so no gate measured contact with static obstacles #ev("6.20"). In one demonstration take an escort drove a robot into a rack island and held it there, a sustained robot-obstacle contact confirmed offline from ground-truth poses and the recording (Chapter 6) #ev("6.01"). The 0.20 m floor limits a push at or near contact rather than preventing the approach #ev("1.04"), and passing the gates is not a general safety claim.

== Report Outline <sec:intro-outline>

*Chapter 2* reviews multi-agent path finding and congestion-aware planning, reactive collision avoidance and Nav2, taxonomies and resolution strategies for deadlock, language and vision-language models as robot planners, and the methods that ground or check their plans. Each section ends with the gap it leaves. A comparison table then sets the closest systems against this report along five axes (@sec:bg-comparison), and the chapter closes with the combined research gap (@sec:bg-gap).

*Chapter 3* presents the system design: the two-layer architecture, the six modules and their contracts, the detect–decide–ground–act–resolve pipeline, the strategy vocabulary, the deterministic grounding layer and the safety invariants (@sec:design).

*Chapter 4* describes the implementation on the pinned runtime: the software stack, each module as built, the VLM backends and the prompt, the engineering lessons that shaped the simulation platform, testing and reproducibility, the physical TurtleBot3 demonstration, and the later extensions that are not evaluated (@sec:impl).

*Chapter 5* sets out the experimental methodology: the research questions, the history of the preregistration from v1 to v3, the simulated scenes, the arms, the registered measures, the run procedure and safety gates, the analysis plan, the offline bench, the offline analyses, the demonstration campaign, and the threats to validity known in advance (@sec:ch5-rq).

*Chapter 6* reports the results: the IB10 comparisons, the prereg-v3 tranche-1 pilot, the failure-cause classification and interception analysis of the five-robot episodes, the exploratory findings (presentation order, model refusals, the offline bench), the head-on decision analyses (re-score and offline replays), the plan-influence trace, the demonstration campaign, the safety and contact findings, and the physical demonstration.

*Chapter 7* discusses what the evidence supports and what it does not, discusses where the model's proposals stop, in grounding refusals and in how little of its plans reaches execution, and sets out the limitations and the threats to validity, including those that arose after the design was fixed.

*Chapter 8* concludes the report and outlines future work.

An appendix, the Evidence Index, maps every number in the text to the file or commit that supports it.
